#====================================================================
# DRAFT - Upstream CMake test framework fetched at configure time
# contributor: W082 (wanderlust_0823)
#====================================================================

# ═══ Layer 0: 错误面 (Error Surface) ═══
id:                     DRAFT-cmake-test-framework-fetch-to-conan-find-package
trigger_tool:           cmake|conan
phase:                  configuration
error_signature:        By not providing "FindCatch2.cmake" in CMAKE_MODULE_PATH|Could not find a package configuration file provided by "(Catch2|GTest|doctest)"|Catch2Config.cmake.*not found

# ═══ Layer 1: 识别层 (Recognition) ═══
severity:               2
category:               build-system, cmake, cmakedeps, conan-recipe, testing
prescan_pattern:        FetchContent_Declare\s*\(\s*(Catch2|catch2|GTest|googletest|doctest|GoogleTest)
prescan_globs:          CMakeLists.txt, *.cmake
match_keywords:         Catch2, GoogleTest, FetchContent, ExternalProject_Add, find_package, CMakeDeps, offline CI, run_upstream_tests
title:                  Feed the upstream CMake test framework in as a packaged dependency instead of a configure-time download

# ═══ Layer 2: 诊断层 (Diagnosis) ═══
root_cause:             Upstream CMake projects declare their test framework inside the test-option branch (FetchContent_Declare(Catch2 URL https://github.com/...) plus FetchContent_MakeAvailable), so merely enabling the upstream suite makes CMake configure depend on an external code host. The package repository already publishes those frameworks, but rewriting the fetch into find_package(Catch2 REQUIRED) is not sufficient on its own: Conan generates no Catch2Config.cmake unless the recipe calls CMakeDeps(self).generate(), so the rewritten configure fails with the error signature above.
affected_scope:         Conan recipes whose upstream test target downloads Catch2, GoogleTest or a similar framework during configure, in particular header-only C++ libraries that must keep running the real upstream suite in CI.
verification_cmd:       grep -RnE 'FetchContent_Declare|ExternalProject_Add' <source>/CMakeLists.txt <source>/test/CMakeLists.txt; conan create <recipe> 2>&1 | grep -E 'FindCatch2|Could not find a package configuration'; conan test test_package <reference>

# ═══ Layer 3: 方案层 (Solution Space) ═══
rec_priority:           1
rec_approach:           packaged-test-framework-with-cmadeps-generation
rec_description:        Keep the upstream suite enabled and move the framework from a network fetch to a package input in three coupled steps: require the repository-published framework gated behind the same upstream-test option; patch only the fetch lines into find_package(<TestFramework> REQUIRED) through the recipe patch pipeline (refresh-patch --auto, no test-logic change); and generate the CMake config package with CMakeDeps(self).generate() before the toolchain. Gating the requirement on the option keeps the shipped package dependency-free, so a header-only cleared package_id stays unchanged and consumers never see a test dependency.
rec_command:            python3 scripts/ohc-run.py refresh-patch <name> <version> --auto; conanfile.py requirements(): self.requires("catch2/2.13.10-r0") when run_upstream_tests / generate(): CMakeDeps(self).generate(); conan create .
rec_auto_fix:           false
rec_impl:               conan-recipe

fallback_priority:      2
fallback_approach:      fetchcontent-source-dir-injection
fallback_description:   When the framework is not published in the package repository, keep the upstream fetch declaration intact and steer it at a checksum-verified local source tree through the FetchContent injection variable. This avoids patching upstream CMake logic altogether, but the local tree must come from an approved, recorded source.
fallback_command:       cmake -DFETCHCONTENT_SOURCE_DIR_CATCH2=<approved-local-catch2-source> <source>; ctest --output-on-failure
fallback_auto_fix:      false
fallback_impl:          manual

forbid_1_pattern:       FetchContent[^|]*\|\|[[:space:]]*true|mirror[^ ]*(Catch2|googletest)[^ ]*\.zip
forbid_1_reason:        Swallowing the download failure or re-pointing it at an arbitrary mirror keeps package reproducibility dependent on an unverified third-party artifact instead of the audited package repository.
forbid_1_consequence:   CI passes only while an outside host happens to serve the file, and the test evidence cannot be reproduced from the recipe alone.
forbid_2_pattern:       find_package\((Catch2|GTest)[^)]*RESULT_VARIABLE[^|]*\n?[^O]|BUILD_TESTING"?[[:space:]]*=?[[:space:]]*"?OFF.*run_upstream_tests[[:space:]]*=[[:space:]]*True
forbid_2_reason:        Making find_package quietly optional, or switching the suite off while still claiming upstream tests, removes the validation layer instead of relocating its input.
forbid_2_consequence:   The PR reports an upstream test pass with zero upstream cases actually executed, and the reviewer cannot tell from the log that the suite was dropped.
forbid_3_pattern:       sed -i[^;]*CMakeLists\.txt|self\.run\(["']patch .*CMakeLists
forbid_3_reason:        Editing upstream build files outside the recipe patch pipeline leaves no reviewable diff and cannot be re-applied deterministically from a clean source tree.
forbid_3_consequence:   The build is no longer reproducible from conandata.yml plus patches/, and the next recipe refresh silently loses the fix.

# ═══ Layer 4: 原则引用 ═══
principles:             []

